A witness for rule J12's quotation exemption: one attributed quotation, which must not be
reported, and three near-misses which must be.

The exemption needs BOTH an attributing verb and a code span, because it is the one place in
this rule where failing to recognise something lets a live figure through. The three below
each have one half and not the other, and each carries a different figure - the clause
reports a row and a claim, so three identical claims would collapse into one message and the
test would pass while two of the three had stopped working.

    The row said `45 covered source files are compared whole`, which stopped being true.

Not exempt: a code span with no attributing verb before it. This is how a row would evade
the clause by quoting itself.

    The comparison covers `12 covered source files` in the current tree.

Not exempt: an attributing verb with no code span. Reported speech is not a quotation, and
the figure in it is the row's own.

    The row said that 78 covered source files are compared whole.

Not exempt: the verb is too far from the span. A sentence that wanders between the two is
not attributing the one to the other.

    The row said something that took a very long time indeed to write down and explain at
    the length it deserved, concerning `93 covered source files`.
